V=[
VP(C,'2–3',S('2','3'),'“YOU RELY ON OTHERS”',
 sc('Shumbha alone in his chariot pointing angrily at the Goddess surrounded by the Mothers and Kali',BATTLE+CHARIOT(50,140,1.0)+ASURA(50,104,.8,'#3a5a6a')+SPEECH(70,30,'not fair!',80,11)+DEVI(160,0,.5,False)+''.join(MOTHER(i,120+(i%4)*24,60+(i//4)*40,.35) for i in range(7))),
 'Seeing his brother Niśumbha, dear as his own life, slain, and his army being slaughtered, Śumbha said in anger: “Durgā, puffed up with pride in your strength, don’t be so arrogant. Most haughty one, you fight relying on the strength of others.”',
 [('prāṇa-sammitam','<b>dear as life</b>'),('bala-avalepa','<b>pride in strength</b>'),('garvam mā āvaha','<b>don’t be arrogant</b>'),('anyāsām balam āśritya','<b>relying on the strength of others</b>'),('ati-māninī','<b>most haughty</b>')],
 '<b>The tyrant’s complaint.</b> Śumbha has lost everything, and now calls it unfair: “You have helpers!” It is the voice of the ego that sees only separate beings. Her answer is the philosophical heart of the whole text.',
 'The ego sees <b>many separate powers</b>.'),
VP(C,'5–6',S('5','6'),'“I AM ALONE IN THIS WORLD”',
 sc('The Seven Mothers and Kali flowing as streams of light back into the body of the Goddess, who stands alone and radiant',GLOW(110,70,70)+DEVI(110,10,.8,False)+''.join(f'<g opacity=".55">{MOTHER(i,20+i*30,90,.38)}</g><path d="M{20+i*30} 96 Q{(20+i*30+110)/2:.0f} 60 110 60" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"/>' for i in range(7))),
 'The Devī said: “I am alone here in the world. Who else is there besides me? See, wicked one, these, my own powers, entering into me.” Then all those goddesses, Brahmāṇī and the rest, dissolved into the body of the Devī. Ambikā alone remained.',
 [('ekā eva aham','<b>I alone</b>'),('dvitīyā kā','<b>what second?</b>'),('mat-vibhūtayaḥ','<b>my own powers, my manifestations</b>'),('layam jagmuḥ','<b>dissolved</b>'),('ekā eva āsīt','<b>she alone remained</b>')],
 '<b>The great answer.</b> “Ekaivāhaṃ jagatyatra, dvitīyā kā mamāparā”: I am alone; what second is there? The Mothers were never outside helpers. They were her own forms, and she draws them back. This is the non-dual (advaita) teaching of the Devī Māhātmya in one line.',
 '“I alone exist; <b>who else is there?</b>”'),
VP(C,'8',S('8'),'“STAND FIRM”',
 sc('The Goddess, alone and still, facing Shumbha across the battlefield; a dotted circle shows the many forms she has gathered into herself',BATTLE+'<circle cx="150" cy="70" r="56" fill="none" stroke="#e0a800" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>'+DEVI(150,0,.75)+CHARIOT(50,140,.9)+ASURA(50,104,.7,'#3a5a6a')+SPEECH(150,150,'sthiro bhava',90,10)),
 'The Devī said: “The many forms in which I stood here by my power, I have withdrawn. Now I stand in battle alone. Stand firm.”',
 [('vibhūtyā','<b>by my power</b>'),('bahubhiḥ rūpaiḥ','<b>in many forms</b>'),('saṃhṛtam','<b>withdrawn</b>'),('tiṣṭhāmi ājau','<b>I stand in battle</b>'),('sthiraḥ bhava','<b>stand firm!</b>')],
 '<b>One against one.</b> She removes Śumbha’s excuse. If he wanted a fair fight, here it is: the One against the one who thinks he is the greatest.',
 'She gathers her forms and stands <b>alone</b>.'),
VP(C,'12–13',S('12','13'),'DIVINE WEAPONS',
 sc('Blazing divine missiles crossing in mid-air between the Goddess and Shumbha, hers breaking his with a HUM',NIGHTSKY+STARS(10,2)+DEVI(170,10,.55)+CHARIOT(50,140,.9)+ASURA(50,104,.7,'#3a5a6a')+''.join(f'<path d="M{70+k*20} {60+k*6} l20 -4" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="3"/>' for k in range(4))+T(110,40,'HUṂ!',16,'#ffd60a')),
 'The divine missiles that Ambikā let fly in hundreds, the lord of the daityas broke with counter-weapons. And the divine missiles he released, the Supreme Goddess broke as if in play, with fierce sounds like “huṃ”.',
 [('divya astra','<b>divine missiles</b>'),('pratīghāta','<b>counter-weapons</b>'),('babhañja','<b>broke</b>'),('līlayā eva','<b>as if in play</b>'),('huṃ-kāra','<b>the sound “huṃ”</b>')],
 '<b>A real duel.</b> Śumbha is no Dhūmralocana: he can actually counter her weapons. But note the difference. He needs weapons to break hers; she breaks his with a sound, “as if in play”.',
 'He needs weapons; <b>she needs only a sound</b>.'),
VP(C,'14–17',S('14','15','16','17'),'BOW, SPEAR, SWORD, SHIELD',
 sc('Shumbha’s bow snapped, his spear cut by a discus, and his hundred-moon sword and sun-bright shield split by her arrows',BATTLE+DEVI(170,0,.55)+ASURA(50,150,1.0,'#3a5a6a')+'<path d="M80 50 q-10 20 0 40" stroke="#7a5a3a" stroke-width="4" fill="none"/><path d="M76 66 l8 6" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="3"/>'+'<circle cx="110" cy="70" r="12" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111"/>'+SWORD(110,140,-30,.8)+'<circle cx="130" cy="120" r="12" fill="#fff8c0" stroke="#111"/>'),
 'Then the asura covered the Devī with hundreds of arrows; and she, angered, cut his bow with her arrows. His bow cut, the daitya king took up a spear; the Devī cut that too with her discus while it was still in his hand. Then the lord of the daitya lords seized a shining sword and a shield with a hundred moons and rushed at the Devī. As he came on, Caṇḍikā at once cut his sword with sharp arrows from her bow, and his shield, bright as the sun’s rays.',
 [('śara-śataiḥ','<b>with hundreds of arrows</b>'),('dhanuḥ ciccheda','<b>cut the bow</b>'),('kare sthitām','<b>still in his hand</b>'),('śata-candram','<b>with a hundred moons</b>'),('arka-kara-amalam','<b>bright as the sun’s rays</b>')],
 '<b>Stripped, piece by piece.</b> Like Mahiṣa’s forms, Śumbha’s weapons are taken away one at a time: bow, spear, sword, shield. Each is the next thing he relies on.',
 'One by one, <b>everything he relies on is cut</b>.'),
VP(C,'18–21',S('18','20','21'),'BARE FISTS',
 sc('Shumbha, horses and chariot gone, punching the Goddess in the chest; she slaps him back and he falls flat',BATTLE+'<path d="M20 150 l30 -20" stroke="#7a5a3a" stroke-width="4"/>'+ASURA(80,150,1.0,'#3a5a6a')+'<circle cx="110" cy="80" r="8" fill="#7a9a5a" stroke="#111"/>'+DEVI(160,0,.6,False)+'<path d="M120 60 l-14 10" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="3"/>'+T(110,30,'SLAP!',14,'#c0392b')),
 'She felled his horses and his chariot with its charioteer. Horses slain, bow cut, without a charioteer, the daitya seized a terrible hammer, set on killing Ambikā; as it fell she cut it with sharp arrows. Even so he rushed at her, fist raised, and the bull among daityas brought his fist down on the Devī’s heart. And the Devī struck him on the chest with the flat of her hand; struck by that slap, he fell to the ground.',
 [('hata-aśvaḥ','<b>horses slain</b>'),('mudgara','<b>a hammer, club</b>'),('muṣṭim udyamya','<b>raising his fist</b>'),('hṛdaye','<b>on the heart</b>'),('tala-prahāra','<b>a slap with the open hand</b>')],
 '<b>Fist against palm.</b> Stripped of everything, Śumbha still fights with his bare fist. She answers with an open hand, a slap, not a blow. The mightiest tyrant of the three worlds is knocked flat by a slap.',
 'His fist meets her <b>open hand</b>.'),
VP(C,'22–24',S('22','23','24'),'A FIGHT IN THE SKY',
 sc('Shumbha leaping into the sky holding the Goddess; the two wrestling among the clouds with nothing beneath them, sages watching amazed',NIGHTSKY+STARS(14,4)+'<ellipse cx="60" cy="140" rx="40" ry="10" fill="#555"/><ellipse cx="170" cy="140" rx="40" ry="10" fill="#555"/>'+ASURA(90,90,.9,'#3a5a6a')+DEVI(130,0,.55,False)+SAGE(30,156,.4)+SAGE(196,156,.4)+T(30,120,'!',14,'#fff')+T(196,120,'!',14,'#fff')),
 'The daitya king suddenly rose again, leapt up, seized the Devī and went high into the sky. There too Caṇḍikā, without any support, fought him. Then the daitya and Caṇḍikā fought hand to hand in the sky, a combat such as never was before, which amazed the siddhas and sages.',
 [('utpatya','<b>leaping up</b>'),('gaganam āsthitaḥ','<b>went into the sky</b>'),('nirādhārā','<b>without support</b>'),('niyuddha','<b>hand-to-hand combat</b>'),('vismaya-kāraka','<b>causing amazement</b>')],
 '<b>Nirādhārā.</b> She fights in the sky “without support”, standing on nothing. That one word says who she is: she needs no ground, because she is the ground of everything (“jagat-pratiṣṭhā”, chapter 5).',
 'She fights in the sky, <b>standing on nothing</b>.'),
VP(C,'25–28',S('25','26','27','28'),'ŚUMBHA FALLS',
 sc('The Goddess whirling Shumbha around and hurling him to earth, then piercing his chest with her trident; the earth, oceans and mountains shaking as he falls',HIMA+'<path d="M0 140 q55 -10 110 0 t110 0 V160 H0z" fill="#2a6ab0" stroke="#111"/>'+ASURA(110,140,1.2,'#3a5a6a')+DEVI(110,-30,.6)+'<path d="M110 50 L110 100" stroke="#111" stroke-width="4"/>'+'<path d="M20 20 a30 30 0 1 1 40 0" fill="none" stroke="#111" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>'+T(110,156,'THE EARTH SHAKES',10)),
 'After long fighting with him, Ambikā lifted him up, whirled him around and hurled him to the ground. Thrown down, reaching the earth, the evil one raised his fist and rushed at her again, wanting to kill Caṇḍikā. As the lord of all the daityas came on, the Devī pierced his chest with her trident and felled him to the earth. Pierced by the point of the Devī’s trident, he fell lifeless to the ground, shaking the whole earth with its oceans, islands and mountains.',
 [('bhrāmayāmāsa','<b>whirled around</b>'),('cikṣepa dharaṇī-tale','<b>hurled to the ground</b>'),('vakṣasi','<b>in the chest</b>'),('gata-asuḥ','<b>lifeless</b>'),('sa-abdhi-dvīpām sa-parvatām','<b>with oceans, islands and mountains</b>')],
 '<b>He never gives up.</b> Thrown to the ground, Śumbha rises again with his fist. The ego fights to the very last breath. Her trident ends it, and his fall shakes the whole earth: so big was the place he took up.',
 'The last of the tyrants <b>falls, and the earth shakes</b>.'),
VP(C,'29–32',S('29','30','31','32'),'THE WORLD AT PEACE',
 sc('A clear blue sky with a bright sun, calm rivers flowing, gods singing and apsaras dancing, gentle breezes',
  '<rect width="220" height="160" fill="#bfe3ff"/>'+SUN(180,34,18)+'<path d="M0 120 q50 -16 110 0 t110 0 V160 H0z" fill="#4aa3df" stroke="#111"/>'+GODS(110,.45,3,30,30)+''.join(f'<g transform="rotate({-10+k*10} {130+k*26} 100)">'+DEVI(130+k*26,60,.25,False,'#e05a8a')+'</g>' for k in range(3))+''.join(f'<path d="M{20+k*40} 30 q10 -6 20 0" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>' for k in range(3))),
 'When that evil one was slain, the whole universe became serene and gained perfect health, and the sky became clear. The portentous clouds and meteors that had appeared before were calmed, and the rivers flowed in their courses when he was felled. Then all the hosts of gods were overjoyed at his death; the Gandharvas sang sweetly, others played music and the Apsarases danced. Pure winds blew, and the sun shone brightly.',
 [('prasannam akhilam','<b>all became serene</b>'),('svāsthyam','<b>health, well-being</b>'),('nirmalam nabhaḥ','<b>a clear sky</b>'),('utpāta-megha','<b>portentous clouds</b>'),('mārga-vāhinyaḥ','<b>flowing in their courses</b>'),('puṇyāḥ vātāḥ','<b>pure winds</b>')],
 '<b>Nature heals.</b> The last verses say nothing about armies. They say the sky cleared, rivers found their courses, winds blew pure and the sun shone. When tyranny ends, the world itself becomes healthy again.',
 'When the tyrant falls, <b>the whole world heals</b>.'),
]
